{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1410,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you’ve heard only one thing about black\nholes, it’s probably that, once inside a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6180,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole’s event horizon, nothing, not\neven light, can escape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10830,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At which point it’s natural to wonder, if\nnothing can escape a black hole, how could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14830,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we ever observe them?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16260,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do we even know they exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18150,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, only things inside the event horizon\nare stuck – black holes also gravitationally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23320,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pull on stuff outside their event horizons,\nand by looking at that stuff we can get a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27660,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really good sense that there’s a black hole\nnearby."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30160,
    "dDurationMs": 4969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, lots and lots of stars orbit\nin pairs , but we also see stars orbiting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35129,
    "dDurationMs": 6101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things that aren’t normal stars, but instead\nemit crazy amounts of x-rays – and x-rays"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41230,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in space often come from dust and gas that\ngets superheated while spiraling into a very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46370,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dense, very heavy object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48230,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, by figuring out the mass and orbital\ncharacteristics of the stars whose partners"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52879,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emit x-rays, we can determine how heavy the\npartners are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57260,
    "dDurationMs": 3959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some parters are lightweight enough to be\nneutron stars , but neutron stars can only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61219,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get so big before they collapse in on themselves\n– theoretical calculations put their upper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65550,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size limit at around 2-3 times the mass of\nthe sun, and the biggest ones we’ve observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69740,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all fall inside that limit . And yet, there\nare plenty of stars whose orbits clearly show"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74680,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that their x-ray-emitting partners are 5-10\ntimes the mass of the sun, and we simply don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79660,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know anything else these could be other than\nblack holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83000,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes you don’t even need an orbiting\nstar at all, and just the x-rays and radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86890,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves from the hot infalling material can\nbe used to determine the mass of a solitary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90650,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-star object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92310,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In some cases they turn out to be neutron\nstars, but in others they turn out to be way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96140,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too heavy, and can only be black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99450,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are also objects at the centers of lots\nof galaxies (including our own), that emit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104050,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lots of x-rays, radio waves and infrared radiation,\nbut not much visible light, and we know these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109370,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects are stupendously heavy because of\nthe way that nearby stars and hot glowing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113650,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dust orbit them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115140,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These orbits tell us the objects are both\nso heavy and so small they can’t possibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119600,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be a star or cluster of stars or distributed\nclumps of other invisible matter; the only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124680,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing they could be is supermassive black\nholes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128650,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in the middle of the Milky Way\nthere’s an x-ray, radio wave and infrared-emitting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134040,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object called “Sagittarius A*” with nearby\nstars orbiting it in such such small, fast"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139700,
    "dDurationMs": 4090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbits that we know it weighs 4 million times\nas much as the sun!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143790,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally, we’ve also directly observed,\non multiple occasions, gravitational waves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148370,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that were emitted from the inspiralling collisions\nof two very heavy dense objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152440,
    "dDurationMs": 3710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of those waves have the signature of\na collision between objects lightweight enough"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156150,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157820,
    "dDurationMs": 3821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But other waves could only have come from\ncollisions between objects far too heavy to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161641,
    "dDurationMs": 4349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be anything but pairs of black holes merging\nto become single, bigger, black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165990,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in these cases, the details of the wave\nsignatures looked exactly like what theoretical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170510,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole collision calculations predict."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172790,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, in many different places throughout the\nuniverse, we’ve detected very dense high-mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177240,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects by their gravity – either indirectly\nvia their affect on nearby bright stuff like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stars or accretion disks of gas and dust,\nor directly via their gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187000,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of these dense high-mass things are too\ndark to be regular stars, too compact AND"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192120,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too dark to be clusters of stars, and too\nheavy to be neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196170,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They exist, they behave pretty much exactly\nthe way physics predicts black holes would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200500,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "act, and there’s literally nothing else\nthey could be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203370,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To quote an astronomer: we have “strong\nconfidence that black holes, or at least objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208150,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that have many of the features of black holes,\nexist”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211150,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, if it looks like a black hole\nand acts like a black hole… we call it a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215319,
    "dDurationMs": 2441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217760,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to NASA's James Webb Space Telescope\nProject at the Space Telescope Science Institute"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222310,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for supporting this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223720,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The James Webb Space Telescope will be able\nto observe the most distant emissions from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227530,
    "dDurationMs": 4429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of the earliest supermassive black holes\nin primordial galaxies and hopefully help"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231959,
    "dDurationMs": 4071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us understand how black holes drive galaxy\nevolution and development."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236030,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Webb will also spot black holes via the stars,\ngas and dust they attract, and help us understand"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241400,
    "dDurationMs": 6419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole energy dynamics, including the\npowerful relativistic jets they can produce."
    } ]
  } ]
}
